pkg:Evidence_v1_6_1 a quality:Evidence, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Gate transcript for code-abundance-rdodi v1.6.1 (PATCH release)"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "PATCH: documentation-only. User asked whether BRSF distinguishes functional from non-functional product scope the way it distinguishes product scope from work scope. Checked directly against backlog_tbox_v1_62_0.ttl: ScopeLayer (Layer_Product/Layer_Work, PMBOK-sourced) is a real, closed two-member enumeration; searched the whole TBox for functional/non-functional/FURPS/ISO-25010/quality-attribute vocabulary and found none (only the unrelated OWL reserved term FunctionalProperty). Confirmed this is a real gap, not a preference, using this session's own register as evidence: its 3 Layer_Product-tagged Deliverables are demonstrably heterogeneous when re-read fresh -- 2 are quality/non-functional claims (gate-verification reliability; disclosure transparency) and 1 is a functional capability claim (instantiates a specific pattern) -- all three currently indistinguishable under one tag. Filed 00-proposals/Proposal_BRSF_ProductScopeKind_v1_0_0.md proposing a sibling ProductScopeKind enumeration (Kind_Functional/Kind_NonFunctional) domained to ScopeDeliverable only, citing ISO/IEC 25010 as a starting-point external anchor (alternative IEEE 830/29148 noted, no position taken on which the framework owner should prefer). Not implemented in BRSF's own files -- this session has no authority there and never edited one (verified: 0 commits from this session touch backlog-roadmap-framework/)."@en ;
    pub:transcriptSHA256 "bc870e66d11d1a1ee00453d9ba6738fbbe62880d0bd16d54cac33e3f2a357009"^^xsd:string .
